WEBVTT

00:00:00.585 --> 00:00:04.422
Imagine two ways to push a puck into a scoring region One leaves the

00:00:04.434 --> 00:00:07.907
puck near the center of the table The other sends it into a pocket that

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:12.813
the robot cannot reach Both produce the same immediate task reward For

00:00:12.825 --> 00:00:16.699
a single trial they look equally good but for a robot that must keep

00:00:16.711 --> 00:00:21.192
practicing one action destroys the next trial On the graph color the

00:00:21.204 --> 00:00:25.746
task goals gold and the starting region green One gold node has a path

00:00:25.758 --> 00:00:30.676
back to green the other is an absorbing trap A task critic trained only

00:00:30.688 --> 00:00:35.108
for reaching gold has no reason to prefer the reversible route We need

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.885
a second quantity that asks about the future after the apparent success

00:00:39.286 --> 00:00:43.317
Leave no Trace introduces a learned reset policy alongside the forward

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:47.664
policy The reset policy does more than clean up afterward Its value

00:00:47.676 --> 00:00:51.914
function judges proposed forward actions before execution This turns

00:00:51.927 --> 00:00:56.456
recovery into a learned constraint on exploration We will separate the

00:00:56.468 --> 00:01:00.475
exact action gate from the uncertainty in its estimates because that

00:01:00.488 --> 00:01:03.232
uncertainty determines whether the gate is useful

00:01:05.193 --> 00:01:09.370
Define a forward reward for accomplishing the task and a reset reward

00:01:09.383 --> 00:01:13.196
for returning to states near the initial distribution These are two

00:01:13.208 --> 00:01:17.009
control problems with shared physical dynamics They may use different

00:01:17.021 --> 00:01:21.453
rewards different policies and different critics A backward policy is

00:01:21.465 --> 00:01:26.310
not generally the forward policy with its actions negated contact friction

00:01:26.529 --> 00:01:31.180
and object geometry make literal time reversal unreliable The training

00:01:31.192 --> 00:01:35.053
loop alternates the two controllers The forward policy starts from the

00:01:35.066 --> 00:01:39.619
state actually produced by the preceding reset attempt The reset policy

00:01:39.631 --> 00:01:43.141
starts from the state actually produced by the forward attempt Their

00:01:43.153 --> 00:01:46.917
data distributions are therefore coupled As the forward policy becomes

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:52.018
adventurous it creates new recovery problems As the reset policy improves

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:56.693
it permits more useful exploration The paper uses off-policy learning

00:01:56.996 --> 00:02:00.348
which can reuse transitions for both objectives when the appropriate

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.574
rewards are available That is an efficiency opportunity not a license

00:02:04.586 --> 00:02:08.848
to mix reward labels indiscriminately The same action may be good for

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:12.637
putting the puck in the goal and bad for returning it to the start Each

00:02:12.649 --> 00:02:15.284
critic must retain the meaning of its own return

00:02:17.263 --> 00:02:21.319
Suppose the puck is one centimeter from the table edge The reset policy

00:02:21.331 --> 00:02:25.095
can pull it inward so the state value under that policy may be high

00:02:25.508 --> 00:02:29.066
But the forward policy proposes a push outward If we check only the

00:02:29.078 --> 00:02:33.001
state value we approve an action using evidence about a different action

00:02:33.414 --> 00:02:37.664
The danger occurs in the transition we failed to evaluate An action

00:02:37.676 --> 00:02:42.691
value solves this mismatch The reset Q function asks what reset return

00:02:42.703 --> 00:02:46.468
would I expect if I first took this particular proposed action and then

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.329
followed the reset policy The initial action is supplied by the forward

00:02:50.341 --> 00:02:54.676
controller The continuation is supplied by the backward controller This

00:02:54.689 --> 00:02:58.805
mixed interpretation is exactly what makes the score relevant before

00:02:58.817 --> 00:03:03.152
execution The early-abort rule compares that score to a threshold If

00:03:03.165 --> 00:03:07.014
the predicted reset return is too low the proposed forward action is

00:03:07.026 --> 00:03:11.543
rejected and the reset controller takes over The robot has not yet entered

00:03:11.556 --> 00:03:15.988
the successor state This timing matters deciding to recover after an

00:03:16.061 --> 00:03:20.420
irreversible transition is too late even if the recovery detector is

00:03:20.432 --> 00:03:21.926
perfectly accurate afterward

00:03:23.894 --> 00:03:27.865
A common shorthand calls the reset critic a probability of recovery

00:03:28.278 --> 00:03:32.370
That can be a useful intuition but we should derive the conditions under

00:03:32.382 --> 00:03:36.341
which it is literally true In this teaching example give a reward of

00:03:36.353 --> 00:03:41.004
one on the first successful reset transition zero elsewhere and stop

00:03:41.016 --> 00:03:45.497
the reset return at success Let tau count transitions until that success

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:50.209
The return is gamma raised to tau minus one if success occurs and zero

00:03:50.318 --> 00:03:54.556
if it never occurs Its expectation combines success probability with

00:03:54.568 --> 00:03:59.085
the time needed to succeed When gamma is one this becomes a recovery

00:03:59.097 --> 00:04:03.785
probability When gamma is smaller two policies with identical eventual

00:04:03.797 --> 00:04:07.440
success rates can have different values because one takes longer to

00:04:07.452 --> 00:04:12.042
return The paper also studies tasks with other reset rewards so its

00:04:12.054 --> 00:04:16.511
numerical thresholds need not lie between zero and one A threshold of

00:04:16.523 --> 00:04:21.162
point seven has no universal meaning independent of reward scale discount

00:04:21.453 --> 00:04:25.545
and termination convention In our lab we use an explicitly declared

00:04:25.557 --> 00:04:29.965
discounted first-success model That makes the displayed scores interpretable

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:33.936
without pretending they reproduce the paper's continuous-control critics

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:40.636
Visualize the reset value as a landscape over possible successor situations

00:04:41.036 --> 00:04:44.873
Near the starting region returning is easy and the landscape is high

00:04:45.335 --> 00:04:50.678
Farther away obstacles and uncertainty create valleys A threshold draws

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:55.001
a contour through that landscape For a fixed critic actions below the

00:04:55.013 --> 00:04:59.385
contour are rejected More precisely the contour belongs to state-action

00:04:59.397 --> 00:05:03.744
space even when we draw a two-dimensional slice Now improve the reset

00:05:03.756 --> 00:05:07.982
policy It learns to reach around an obstacle and recover from states

00:05:07.994 --> 00:05:12.499
that previously defeated it Their reset values can rise expanding the

00:05:12.512 --> 00:05:16.082
actions permitted to the forward learner The constraint is learned and

00:05:16.094 --> 00:05:19.992
changes with competence This is different from a fixed geometric wall

00:05:20.004 --> 00:05:24.485
specified by a designer before learning begins But increasing the threshold

00:05:24.606 --> 00:05:28.856
also shrinks exploration If only already-mastered states are allowed

00:05:29.196 --> 00:05:33.252
the system may never collect the data needed to improve recovery elsewhere

00:05:33.665 --> 00:05:37.138
This creates a feedback problem the critic controls what it gets to

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:41.218
observe Our visualization lets you move the threshold and the learned

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:45.359
competence separately so you can see two distinct causes of an apparently

00:05:45.371 --> 00:05:46.318
cautious boundary

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:51.804
Consider an unfamiliar action for which three reset critics predict

00:05:51.816 --> 00:05:56.357
point two point five and point eight Their disagreement is important

00:05:56.758 --> 00:06:00.316
A single average hides the possibility that the action is much better

00:06:00.389 --> 00:06:05.185
or much worse than expected Leave no Trace uses a bootstrap-style ensemble

00:06:05.392 --> 00:06:09.314
with differently initialized value functions to obtain a distribution

00:06:09.326 --> 00:06:13.297
of estimates for a query The paper considers three ways to turn those

00:06:13.309 --> 00:06:17.656
estimates into an abort An optimistic gate aborts only when even the

00:06:17.669 --> 00:06:22.113
largest estimate is below the threshold A mean gate compares the average

00:06:22.514 --> 00:06:26.594
A pessimistic gate aborts when even one critic is below the threshold

00:06:27.007 --> 00:06:30.990
With a threshold of point six our example passes the optimistic gate

00:06:31.208 --> 00:06:35.179
but fails the other two These names describe a choice about exploration

00:06:35.264 --> 00:06:39.223
under disagreement They do not establish calibrated confidence intervals

00:06:39.441 --> 00:06:43.570
or formal safety probabilities Every critic could share the same modeling

00:06:43.582 --> 00:06:48.136
error especially outside the training distribution The interactive ensemble

00:06:48.148 --> 00:06:52.083
slider is therefore a sensitivity experiment it shows how the decision

00:06:52.095 --> 00:06:56.223
rule reacts to scores rather than claiming to measure a real robot's

00:06:56.296 --> 00:06:57.644
uncertainty reliably

00:06:59.623 --> 00:07:03.302
Why would we ever choose the optimistic gate Because unfamiliar does

00:07:03.314 --> 00:07:07.491
not mean impossible If a reset policy has never tried a recoverable

00:07:07.504 --> 00:07:11.535
corner of the table low initial estimates can lock that corner out forever

00:07:11.936 --> 00:07:16.101
Allowing some uncertain actions can generate evidence improve the reset

00:07:16.113 --> 00:07:20.375
controller and eventually make the whole system less dependent on manual

00:07:20.388 --> 00:07:24.528
intervention The cost is that hopeful estimates can be wrong An admitted

00:07:24.541 --> 00:07:28.864
action may enter a trap and require a hard reset A pessimistic gate

00:07:28.876 --> 00:07:33.174
reduces exposure to such disagreement but can also block the exploration

00:07:33.187 --> 00:07:37.655
needed to discover a viable route In the paper's small gridworld study

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:42.585
excessive conservatism can prevent useful exploration The continuous-control

00:07:42.598 --> 00:07:47.273
results do not imply one aggregation rule always wins When you inspect

00:07:47.285 --> 00:07:51.826
the lab hold the true illustrative recovery score fixed and change only

00:07:51.839 --> 00:07:55.615
ensemble spread Some rules change their decisions even though the toy

00:07:55.627 --> 00:08:00.157
environment has not changed That isolates epistemic uncertainty from

00:08:00.169 --> 00:08:04.419
physical difficulty It also reveals why a convenient slogan like higher

00:08:04.431 --> 00:08:08.535
uncertainty means stop is a design choice not a theorem supplied by

00:08:08.548 --> 00:08:09.434
the reset critic

00:08:11.402 --> 00:08:15.287
Even a careful gate can make mistakes Once the robot is in a difficult

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:19.938
state Leave no Trace first lets the reset policy attempt recovery If

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:24.030
it repeatedly fails to reach the designated reset region the system

00:08:24.043 --> 00:08:28.305
requests a hard reset The number of attempts is a parameter that trades

00:08:28.317 --> 00:08:32.361
continued autonomous effort against the cost of waiting in an unproductive

00:08:32.373 --> 00:08:36.089
state This is not a proof that the state is physically irreversible

00:08:36.489 --> 00:08:40.424
A different policy might recover or the current policy might succeed

00:08:40.436 --> 00:08:44.929
on its next stochastic attempt Conversely a truly absorbing trap can

00:08:44.941 --> 00:08:49.021
waste an arbitrarily long time if the attempt budget is too generous

00:08:49.434 --> 00:08:53.539
The algorithm needs an operational stopping decision despite not knowing

00:08:53.551 --> 00:08:57.558
the full reachability structure In a toy model with independent attempts

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:02.015
and fixed success probability the chance of all N attempts failing is

00:09:02.027 --> 00:09:06.423
one minus that probability raised to N That equation helps interpret

00:09:06.435 --> 00:09:10.163
the budget but real attempts are correlated and the state can change

00:09:10.563 --> 00:09:14.364
We explicitly label the independence assumption in our illustration

00:09:14.765 --> 00:09:18.554
Repeated failures provide evidence about a controller not a complete

00:09:18.566 --> 00:09:20.193
characterization of the environment

00:09:22.142 --> 00:09:26.550
Read the algorithm as a cycle During forward control sample a proposed

00:09:26.562 --> 00:09:31.310
action and ask the reset critic to score it If it passes execute it

00:09:31.577 --> 00:09:35.633
store the actual transition and improve the relevant learners If it

00:09:35.645 --> 00:09:40.114
fails hand control to the backward policy without executing the rejected

00:09:40.126 --> 00:09:44.461
action A forward time budget can also trigger the handoff During backward

00:09:44.473 --> 00:09:49.075
control act toward the reset region and update from the collected experience

00:09:49.476 --> 00:09:53.653
When the reset attempt ends decide whether another task attempt is useful

00:09:53.884 --> 00:09:57.223
or whether repeated recovery failure warrants external intervention

00:09:57.624 --> 00:10:01.631
Off-policy learning helps reuse the interaction stream but its data

00:10:01.644 --> 00:10:05.967
handling still has to respect which reward and continuation each critic

00:10:06.173 --> 00:10:10.302
is estimating Three counters make the cycle easier to audit forward

00:10:10.314 --> 00:10:15.171
interactions recovery interactions and hard resets A method that reduces

00:10:15.183 --> 00:10:18.863
human intervention by spending many more robot actions may still be

00:10:18.875 --> 00:10:23.040
attractive but that is a tradeoff to measure The algorithm's central

00:10:23.052 --> 00:10:27.156
contribution is making recovery learnable and predictive not making

00:10:27.169 --> 00:10:29.731
the cost of recovery disappear from the experiment

00:10:31.705 --> 00:10:35.566
The title connects resetting with safe exploration and there is a clear

00:10:35.578 --> 00:10:39.634
reason avoiding states from which recovery is impossible can prevent

00:10:39.646 --> 00:10:43.386
some important failures But the mathematical object being estimated

00:10:43.532 --> 00:10:47.503
is reset return It does not automatically represent every condition

00:10:47.515 --> 00:10:51.850
we care about An action could be physically reversible while still damaging

00:10:51.862 --> 00:10:56.440
an object or violating another task constraint There is also a gap between

00:10:56.453 --> 00:11:01.006
the true reset value and a learned approximation Even if high true value

00:11:01.018 --> 00:11:04.759
were the right criterion approving actions using an inaccurate neural

00:11:04.771 --> 00:11:08.924
critic would not establish a formal guarantee Ensemble disagreement

00:11:08.997 --> 00:11:13.113
can be informative but agreement alone cannot rule out systematic error

00:11:13.514 --> 00:11:17.108
Keep the estimated boundary distinct from an actually certified set

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:20.994
of viable states This distinction helps you compare the next papers

00:11:21.006 --> 00:11:25.669
fairly medal focuses on useful starting distributions risk on efficient

00:11:25.681 --> 00:11:29.907
switching and the no-regret paper on a precisely defined reset cost

00:11:29.968 --> 00:11:33.817
under explicit assumptions None of those objectives should silently

00:11:33.829 --> 00:11:37.946
inherit a broader meaning To evaluate a method first state what its

00:11:37.958 --> 00:11:41.237
score predicts and what failure event its measurements count

00:11:43.193 --> 00:11:46.848
In the interactive recovery lab the horizontal coordinate represents

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:51.134
how far a proposed action pushes toward a difficult region A declared

00:11:51.207 --> 00:11:54.984
analytic function assigns that action a toy probability of recovery

00:11:55.227 --> 00:11:59.112
and a recovery duration Discounting combines those quantities into the

00:11:59.125 --> 00:12:03.362
displayed reference score These functions make the exercise reproducible

00:12:03.751 --> 00:12:07.285
they are not learned models from the paper Move the threshold upward

00:12:07.552 --> 00:12:11.158
and watch the permitted part of the action space shrink Then introduce

00:12:11.171 --> 00:12:15.457
critic bias and spread The reference curve stays in place while three

00:12:15.469 --> 00:12:20.314
estimated critics move around it Choose optimistic mean or pessimistic

00:12:20.327 --> 00:12:24.322
aggregation and inspect the gate's decision at the selected action A

00:12:24.334 --> 00:12:28.220
visible gap between reference and estimate illustrates either false

00:12:28.232 --> 00:12:33.198
permission or unnecessary rejection Try to construct both mistakes deliberately

00:12:33.599 --> 00:12:38.116
Make every critic overly optimistic near the edge then make them overly

00:12:38.129 --> 00:12:42.306
pessimistic in an easy region This experiment captures why the method

00:12:42.403 --> 00:12:47.382
is interesting control learning and data coverage interact It also prevents

00:12:47.394 --> 00:12:51.850
an attractive visual contour from being mistaken for an oracle The contour

00:12:51.984 --> 00:12:55.105
is only as meaningful as its objective and its estimates

00:12:57.076 --> 00:13:00.695
The key conceptual move is to train a skill for recovering and then

00:13:00.707 --> 00:13:04.957
use its value before the forward policy acts That couples exploration

00:13:04.970 --> 00:13:08.552
to the ability to continue learning afterward The action-conditioned

00:13:08.564 --> 00:13:13.203
query is essential a state can be recoverable under one action and become

00:13:13.215 --> 00:13:17.586
a trap under another Timing the check before execution makes the difference

00:13:17.598 --> 00:13:22.140
observable We also saw three limits A discounted score need not be a

00:13:22.152 --> 00:13:26.135
literal probability A learned ensemble does not certify correctness

00:13:26.536 --> 00:13:30.786
A failed recovery budget does not prove physical irreversibility These

00:13:30.798 --> 00:13:34.162
are not reasons to discard the method They tell us how to interpret

00:13:34.174 --> 00:13:37.817
it what to measure and where a proposed extension would need additional

00:13:37.829 --> 00:13:42.444
evidence So far the reset policy has tried to return toward the original

00:13:42.456 --> 00:13:45.734
starting region But why should that be the best place to practice from

00:13:45.747 --> 00:13:49.572
throughout learning In the next lesson we will replace a single notion

00:13:49.584 --> 00:13:53.482
of going back with distribution matching A classifier will become a

00:13:53.494 --> 00:13:57.586
reset reward and useful demonstration states will become a curriculum

00:13:57.695 --> 00:13:58.643
for the forward learner
